{"id": "2026-11-26-news-us-proposes-15-overcapacity-tariff-on-swiss-good", "date": "2026-11-26", "stream": "news", "headline": "US proposes 15% overcapacity tariff on Swiss goods", "summary": "USTR found 14 economies' excess-capacity practices actionable under Section 301 and proposed tariffs up to 15% for Switzerland, with comments due 5 January 2027.", "url": "https://www.hklaw.com/en/insights/publications/2026/11/ustr-proposes-tariffs-following-14-section-301", "source_domain": "hklaw.com", "tier": "T2", "tags": [], "topics": ["switzerland", "economy"], "importance": 3, "display_body": "The US Trade Representative (USTR) proposed on Wednesday 25 November an extra tariff of up to 15% on goods from Switzerland and four other economies, after finding that 14 trading partners' \"structural excess capacity\" in manufacturing is actionable under Section 301 of the Trade Act of 1974. Section 301 lets the USTR impose duties on practices it deems \"unreasonable\" and harmful to US commerce. The 14 economies include Switzerland, the EU, China, Japan, Korea and Taiwan, with proposed rates from up to 15% (Switzerland, the EU, Japan, Korea, Taiwan) to up to 35% (Vietnam). Most notices also name a higher alternative rate if USTR judges consultations unsatisfactory, according to a summary by the law firm Holland & Knight. Nothing takes effect yet: written comments are due Tuesday 5 January 2027 and hearings begin Monday 25 January 2027. SRF reports that for Swiss exports this would mean 15% next year instead of the 12.5% now charged under the July 2026 forced-labour tariffs. That is the same level as the EU and within the November 2025 Swiss–US declaration of intent, far below the 39% Switzerland faced in summer 2025. The State Secretariat for Economic Affairs (SECO) rejects the US allegations, SRF reports, and Swissmem called them \"absurd\". These two Section 301 actions largely replace the tariffs the US Supreme Court struck down in February 2026. SRF's economics editor called the new duties \"factually unfounded\".", "why": "Swiss exporters face a further 2.5-point rise from 2027 unless Bern's negotiations produce the binding 15% ceiling it is seeking. The rate would match the EU's, so Swiss firms would at least compete with their main rivals on equal terms.", "thread_id": "2026-11-26-news-us-proposes-15-overcapacity-tariff-on-swiss-good", "first_seen_date": "2026-11-26", "event_date": "2026-11-25", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["USTR", "Switzerland", "SECO", "Section 301"]}
{"id": "2026-11-26-news-brussels-fines-swiss-fragrance-giants-472m", "date": "2026-11-26", "stream": "news", "headline": "Brussels fines Swiss fragrance giants €472m", "summary": "The European Commission fined Givaudan, Firmenich and IFF about 472 million euros for a 2019-2023 fragrance cartel; the UK CMA added 85 million pounds.", "url": "https://ec.europa.eu/commission/presscorner/detail/en/speech_26_2589", "source_domain": "ec.europa.eu", "tier": "T1", "tags": ["official PR"], "topics": ["economy", "switzerland"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "The European Commission fined the Swiss fragrance makers Givaudan and Firmenich and the US group IFF a combined €472 million on Wednesday 25 November for a cartel, and the UK Competition and Markets Authority (CMA) added £85 million the same day. The Commission says the three, which supply about half of Europe's €4 billion-plus market for fragrances in detergents, shampoos and cleaning products, exchanged commercially sensitive information, including on prices, rather than competing. According to Le Temps/AFP, the conduct ran from March 2019 until the Commission's dawn raids in March 2023. In the EU, Givaudan was fined about €252.4 million, Firmenich €137.7 million and IFF €81.9 million. The CMA says the companies shared information to refuse requests from a major customer, Unilever, including for price cuts, agreed not to poach each other's staff on that account, and in some cases met at in-person \"beer and curry\" meetings in London. All three admitted the conduct and settled. Competition chief Teresa Ribera said the Swiss Competition Commission (COMCO) and the US Justice Department also worked on the case.", "why": "fragrances can make up to 10% of the cost of a bottle of detergent or shampoo, the Commission says, so the overcharge may have reached shoppers. COMCO's involvement leaves open whether a Swiss decision follows.", "thread_id": "2026-11-26-news-brussels-fines-swiss-fragrance-giants-472m", "first_seen_date": "2026-11-26", "event_date": "2026-11-25", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Givaudan", "Firmenich", "IFF", "European Commission", "CMA"]}
{"id": "2026-11-26-news-bern-narrows-responsible-business-counter-propos", "date": "2026-11-26", "stream": "news", "headline": "Bern narrows responsible-business counter-proposal", "summary": "The Federal Council sent parliament a corporate sustainability law as indirect counter-proposal to the responsible-business initiative, with due-diligence duties for 30-60 firms and reporting for about 110.", "url": "https://www.admin.ch/fr/newnsb/Prvb4ekrdHsH", "source_domain": "admin.ch", "tier": "T1", "tags": ["official PR"], "topics": ["switzerland", "politics"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "The Federal Council sent parliament its counter-proposal to the popular initiative \"for responsible large companies\" on Wednesday 25 November: a corporate sustainability law that it has narrowed since consultation. The Federal Council says specific human-rights and environmental due-diligence duties would apply to between 30 and 60 large Swiss companies, which must assess their risks and act on them. It promises no stricter rules than the EU's, except on child labour, where Swiss rules are already stricter. Fewer firms would have to report than today: the sustainability-reporting duty would cover about 110 large companies, almost half as many as now. Compared with the consultation draft, the range of sanctions has been narrowed and climate reports no longer go to shareholders' meetings but to an external auditor. Liability rests on ordinary fault-based liability (art. 41 of the Code of Obligations): the injured party carries the burden of proof, and companies are not liable for damage caused solely by their business partners.", "why": "parliament now weighs a law that adds due-diligence duties for a few dozen firms while shrinking reporting for the rest, against an initiative that wants more. That is the choice voters will eventually face.", "thread_id": "2026-11-26-news-bern-narrows-responsible-business-counter-propos", "first_seen_date": "2026-11-26", "event_date": "2026-11-25", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Federal Council", "Responsible Business Initiative"]}
{"id": "2026-11-26-news-accident-insurance-to-cover-drugged-assault-vict", "date": "2026-11-26", "stream": "news", "headline": "Accident insurance to cover drugged assault victims", "summary": "Federal Council sent parliament an amendment to the Accident Insurance Act so health damage from sexual assault counts as an accident even when the victim was drugged.", "url": "https://www.admin.ch/fr/newnsb/f6CupdOvTdWq", "source_domain": "admin.ch", "tier": "T1", "tags": ["official PR"], "topics": ["switzerland", "health"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "The Federal Council sent parliament a bill on Wednesday 25 November to make accident insurance cover victims of sexual violence in every case, including when they were drugged. Under current law, Swiss social insurance counts an injury as an accident only if it is \"sudden\" and involuntary. Some victims of drug-facilitated assault cannot prove suddenness, so they currently get no accident-insurance benefits. The amendment to the Accident Insurance Act (LAA) would always treat health damage from sexual assault or coercion as an accident, even if the victim could not resist or was not aware, covering medical costs and daily allowances for lost earnings. The draft was very well received in consultation between 1 April and 7 July 2026.", "why": "", "thread_id": "2026-11-26-news-accident-insurance-to-cover-drugged-assault-vict", "first_seen_date": "2026-11-26", "event_date": "2026-11-25", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Federal Council", "LAA"]}
{"id": "2026-11-26-news-swiss-jobs-fall-on-the-quarter-as-outlook-dims", "date": "2026-11-26", "stream": "news", "headline": "Swiss jobs fall on the quarter as outlook dims", "summary": "The FSO said Swiss employment rose 1.2% year on year in Q3 2026 but fell 0.3% quarter on quarter, with more firms planning cuts.", "url": "https://www.admin.ch/fr/newnsb/X83dUsjjR4Lp", "source_domain": "admin.ch", "tier": "T1", "tags": ["official PR"], "topics": ["switzerland", "economy"], "importance": 1, "display_body": "Swiss employment grew 1.2% year on year in the third quarter of 2026, to 5.684 million jobs, but fell 0.3% from the previous quarter and companies' hiring plans weakened, the Federal Statistical Office (FSO) said on Thursday 26 November. Seasonally adjusted, companies shed 19,800 jobs in the quarter, 18,200 of them in services. The share of employment in firms planning to cut staff rose to 5.1% from 3.9%, while the share in firms planning to hire fell to 10.6% from 11.3%. Vacancies still rose 3.3% on the year to 93,900, and 34.2% of firms reported difficulty hiring skilled staff. The release follows the 12 November figures showing unemployment by the International Labour Organization's definition at 5.6%.", "why": "", "thread_id": "2026-11-26-news-swiss-jobs-fall-on-the-quarter-as-outlook-dims", "first_seen_date": "2026-11-26", "event_date": "2026-11-26", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Federal Statistical Office"]}
{"id": "2026-11-26-news-kim-tours-nuclear-plants-pushes-tactical-arms", "date": "2026-11-26", "stream": "news", "headline": "Kim tours nuclear plants, pushes tactical arms", "summary": "North Korean state media said Kim Jong Un toured nuclear material production sites and called for wider use of tactical nuclear weapons.", "url": "https://www.dw.com/en/kim-jong-un-tours-nuclear-material-sites-urges-wider-use-of-tactical-nuclear-weapons/a-80055887", "source_domain": "dw.com", "tier": "T2", "tags": ["single-source"], "topics": ["geopolitics", "security"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "North Korean leader Kim Jong Un toured plants that produce nuclear material on Wednesday 25 November and called for wider use of tactical nuclear weapons, state media reported on Thursday. KCNA did not give the location. Photos showed what appeared to be rows of uranium-enrichment centrifuges, and analysts identified small standardised warheads of the Hwasan-31 type. Kim said production capacity for nuclear material is being steadily expanded, and that he would treat any attempt to deny North Korea's nuclear status, written into its constitution in 2023, as a \"declaration of war\". Yonhap counted it as his fourth visit to a nuclear facility since September 2024. It came about a week after Kim did not publicly respond to Donald Trump's offer to meet during the APEC summit in China. South Korea's defence ministry told Pyongyang to comply with UN Security Council resolutions banning its programmes.", "why": "it is the same week Pyongyang expelled the head of Switzerland's office, and Kim's language signals that any future talks would be between nuclear powers, not about disarmament.", "thread_id": "2026-11-26-news-kim-tours-nuclear-plants-pushes-tactical-arms", "first_seen_date": "2026-11-26", "event_date": "2026-11-25", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["North Korea", "Kim Jong Un", "KCNA"]}
{"id": "2026-11-26-news-poland-plans-no-fly-zone-on-ukraine-border", "date": "2026-11-26", "stream": "news", "headline": "Poland plans no-fly zone on Ukraine border", "summary": "Poland's deputy defence minister said a no-fly zone of a few tens of kilometres along the Ukrainian border will start in late December or early January.", "url": "https://www.rfi.fr/fr/europe/20261125-la-pologne-va-instaurer-une-zone-d-exclusion-a%C3%A9rienne-%C3%A0-la-fronti%C3%A8re-ukrainienne", "source_domain": "rfi.fr", "tier": "T2", "tags": ["single-source"], "topics": ["geopolitics", "security"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "Poland will set up a no-fly zone a few tens of kilometres deep along its border with Ukraine so it can identify and, if needed, shoot down Russian military aircraft, deputy defence minister Cezary Tomczyk said on Wednesday 25 November. He said it should take effect at the end of December or early January. Warsaw said on 20 November that at least one Russian missile struck south-eastern Poland during a Russian attack on Ukraine.", "why": "", "thread_id": "2026-11-26-news-poland-plans-no-fly-zone-on-ukraine-border", "first_seen_date": "2026-11-26", "event_date": "2026-11-25", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Poland", "Ukraine", "Russia", "Cezary Tomczyk"]}
{"id": "2026-11-26-news-judges-urge-dropping-netanyahu-bribery-charge", "date": "2026-11-26", "stream": "news", "headline": "Judges urge dropping Netanyahu bribery charge", "summary": "Judges in Netanyahu's corruption trial recommended dropping the bribery count, citing serious doubts; the attorney general said she would review.", "url": "https://www.tagesschau.de/ausland/asien/israel-netanjahu-korruptionsvorwuerfe-100.html", "source_domain": "tagesschau.de", "tier": "T2", "tags": ["single-source"], "topics": ["politics", "world"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "The judges in Benjamin Netanyahu's corruption trial recommended on Wednesday 25 November that prosecutors drop the bribery charge, saying they have \"serious doubts\" it can meet the legal threshold for conviction. Attorney General Gali Baharav-Miara, summoned to the hearing, said she would review the recommendation. The bribery charge concerns the allegation that Netanyahu granted a telecoms company regulatory benefits in return for favourable coverage on a news site. The other two cases involve fraud and breach of trust, and ARD reports the court has signalled scepticism there too. The judges had made the same recommendation earlier, including this summer. Netanyahu called the cases years of \"character assassination\" and said the trial had failed. That is his own view: no charge has been withdrawn, and the trial, under way since May 2020, continues.", "why": "", "thread_id": "2026-11-26-news-judges-urge-dropping-netanyahu-bribery-charge", "first_seen_date": "2026-11-26", "event_date": "2026-11-25", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Benjamin Netanyahu", "Israel", "Gali Baharav-Miara"]}
{"id": "2026-11-26-news-court-voids-report-behind-ramaphosa-impeachment", "date": "2026-11-26", "stream": "news", "headline": "Court voids report behind Ramaphosa impeachment", "summary": "The Western Cape High Court ruled unlawful the parliamentary panel report that had triggered impeachment proceedings against South African President Ramaphosa.", "url": "https://www.rfi.fr/fr/afrique/20261125-afrique-du-sud-le-pr%C3%A9sident-ramaphosa-gagne-son-recours-contre-un-rapport-d-impeachment", "source_domain": "rfi.fr", "tier": "T2", "tags": ["single-source"], "topics": ["politics", "world"], "importance": 1, "display_body": "South Africa's Western Cape High Court ruled on Wednesday 25 November that the parliamentary panel report finding that President Cyril Ramaphosa may have committed serious violations of the constitution was unlawful. That report had led to impeachment proceedings being opened in May 2026, over an affair that has followed him since 2022, and the ruling halts them.", "why": "", "thread_id": "2026-11-26-news-court-voids-report-behind-ramaphosa-impeachment", "first_seen_date": "2026-11-26", "event_date": "2026-11-25", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Cyril Ramaphosa", "South Africa", "Western Cape High Court"]}
{"id": "2026-11-26-news-diesel-tanker-sinks-off-ibiza-one-dead", "date": "2026-11-26", "stream": "news", "headline": "Diesel tanker sinks off Ibiza, one dead", "summary": "The Malta-flagged tanker Med Emre carrying up to 7,000 tonnes of diesel sank northeast of Ibiza, killing one sailor; Spain activated an emergency plan.", "url": "https://www.tagesschau.de/ausland/europa/tanker-ibiza-100.html", "source_domain": "tagesschau.de", "tier": "T2", "tags": ["single-source"], "topics": ["world"], "importance": 1, "display_body": "The Malta-flagged tanker Med Emre, carrying up to 7,000 tonnes of diesel, sank about 46 km north-east of Ibiza overnight into Thursday 26 November, killing one crew member, with 16 others rescued, the Balearic government said. The cause is unknown. A low-level storm warning was in force. Spain activated an emergency plan and sent a specialist vessel and a surveillance aircraft. It is not yet clear whether cargo has leaked. Diesel spreads faster than crude oil but lingers less, so the main risk is a short, intense pollution spike. Greenpeace warned of damage along a whale and dolphin migration route.", "why": "", "thread_id": "2026-11-26-news-diesel-tanker-sinks-off-ibiza-one-dead", "first_seen_date": "2026-11-26", "event_date": "2026-11-26", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Med Emre", "Ibiza", "Spain"]}
{"id": "2026-11-26-news-eu-offers-balkans-single-market-access-before-en", "date": "2026-11-26", "stream": "news", "headline": "EU offers Balkans single-market access before entry", "summary": "At the EU-Western Balkans summit von der Leyen said Montenegro is approaching the end of accession talks and gradual integration dialogues will start early next year.", "url": "https://ec.europa.eu/commission/presscorner/detail/en/statement_26_2591", "source_domain": "ec.europa.eu", "tier": "T1", "tags": ["official PR"], "topics": ["geopolitics", "politics"], "importance": 1, "display_body": "At the EU–Western Balkans summit on Wednesday 25 November, Commission President Ursula von der Leyen said Montenegro is \"approaching the finish line\" of its accession talks and that the EU will open tailored \"gradual integration\" dialogues with each partner early next year. Gradual integration means giving these countries access to parts of the single market before membership. Von der Leyen said the region had delivered 63% of the reform steps due under the EU's Growth Plan, up from 38% a year ago, which could release another €338 million for Albania, Montenegro and North Macedonia. Four Western Balkan partners have joined the Single Euro Payments Area (SEPA), and talks on roaming at domestic rates began last month. EU leaders meet again in Brussels on 26–27 November for a special summit on the 2028–2034 budget and Ukraine.", "why": "", "thread_id": "2026-11-26-news-eu-offers-balkans-single-market-access-before-en", "first_seen_date": "2026-11-26", "event_date": "2026-11-25", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["European Union", "Montenegro", "Ursula von der Leyen", "Western Balkans"]}
